This is a classic Kubernetes orphaned admission webhook problem.

The SR-IOV Operator has been removed, but its ValidatingWebhookConfiguration/MutatingWebhookConfiguration still exists. When you try to delete a SriovNetworkNodePolicy or SriovOperatorConfig, the API server attempts to call:

operator-webhook-service.openshift-sriov-network-operator.svc

but that service no longer exists, so the API server rejects the request.

Step 1: Find the leftover webhook

Run:

oc get validatingwebhookconfigurations
oc get mutatingwebhookconfigurations

Look for entries containing sriov.

For example, you may see something like:

operator-webhook
sriov-operator-webhook

Step 2: Delete the leftover webhook(s)

For each SR-IOV webhook, delete it:

oc delete validatingwebhookconfiguration <webhook-name>
oc delete mutatingwebhookconfiguration <webhook-name>

For example:

oc delete validatingwebhookconfiguration operator-webhook
oc delete mutatingwebhookconfiguration operator-webhook

(Use the exact names from oc get.)

Step 3: Delete the SR-IOV resources

After removing the webhook, retry:

oc delete sriovnetworknodepolicy --all -n openshift-sriov-network-operator

oc delete sriovoperatorconfig default -n openshift-sriov-network-operator

These should now succeed.

If you don't see any SR-IOV webhook

Run:

oc get validatingwebhookconfigurations -o yaml | grep -i -A5 -B5 sriov

oc get mutatingwebhookconfigurations -o yaml | grep -i -A5 -B5 sriov

Share the output, and I'll tell you exactly which webhook object to delete. This is the last piece of cleanup before reinstalling the SR-IOV Operator.
